        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The main purpose of this study is to evaluate the clinical usefulness of multispectral imaging (MSI) to detect early ophthalmological abnormalities in patients with idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH). Investigators will compare the findings obtained by MSI to standard objective clinical methods used for evaluation of IIH including optical coherence tomography (OCT), fundus photography, and Frisen (papilledema) grading. Outcome measures will include qualitative and quantitative assessment of choroidal thickness, choroidal folds, Frisen grade, and retinal fiber layer hemorrhages. Investigators will develop a novel scoring system to quantify the data obtained from MSI to allow for a clinically-useful measure of retinal, choroidal, and optic nerve head abnormalities.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The Annidis RHA™ is a multispectral ophthalmoscope with multi-image software processing that quickly and non-invasively generates detailed, multi-layer retinal images heretofore unachievable even from more complicated and invasive procedures. This study will compare the RHA’s images of posterior eye tissues to those generated by technologies currently in use on the International Space Station (ISS). The study will assess whether the device provides additional insights into intracranial hypertension.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Results for this study aren’t available.
<p>
        </div>

